==English==

===Etymology===
From ''[[embarrass]]'' + ''[[-ed]]''. The adjective is an extension of the past participle.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ɪmˈbæɹ.əst/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Having a feeling of [[shameful]] discomfort.
#: ''After returning from the pool, Aleshia felt significantly better, though she was still slightly '''embarrassed'''.''
# {{lb|en|archaic}} Impeded; obstructed.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1897|author=Henry James|title=What Maisie Knew|passage={{...}} they could let their eyes hover tenderly on the far-off white cliffs that so often had signalled to the '''embarrassed''' English a promise of safety.}}

====Synonyms====
* {{l|en|ashamed}}

====Antonyms====
* {{l|en|honored}}, {{l|en|proud}}

====Derived terms====
* {{l|en|embarrassedly}}

